# ==========================================================================
# The pool image above warms the build cache with `go build ./...` on the checkout as it
# came from upstream, and the faults are injected after that, so every object in that
# cache describes the code the agent is being asked to reconstruct. GOCACHE sits outside
# /workspace/repo, where neither the flattening nor the git snapshot below reaches it, and the
# pool image hands it to uid 1000: `go tool objdump` on one of those objects gives the
# original function straight back. Empty the cache here, check that it really is empty,
# and re-warm it on the FAULTED tree so the agent still starts warm. The module cache is
# kept for third-party dependencies, which a rollout has no network to fetch again.
#
# BUT NOT THIS REPOSITORY'S OWN MODULES. A nested module that requires the root module at
# a published version (gocron's mocks/, revive's tools/, sh's moreinterp/) makes the
# pool's `go mod download all` put that release in the module cache: this repository's
# own source as upstream published it, as plain .go files under GOMODCACHE and as a zip
# under cache/download. Gate 9 refused gocron, revive and sh for it (PR 362 review).
# Every module path a go.mod in the tree declares is removed from the module cache, every
# version: the extracted source, and under cache/download everything but the .mod, .info
# and list files (the zip, its ziphash, locks). Those three stay. They are metadata, not
# source, and the build needs them offline when an older dependency requires an older
# release of this repository, because Go then reads that release's go.mod for the module
# graph. The re-warm keeps the network for third-party modules, which a nested module may
# need and the pool never fetched, but is refused this repository's own: GONOPROXY sends
# those paths to their VCS and GOVCS turns the VCS off for them, so a module that needs
# one fails with "GOVCS disallows" instead of downloading the source straight back and
# compiling it into the cache. GOVCS is matched against the REPOSITORY ROOT, and
# github.com/go-co-op/gocron/v2 does not match github.com/go-co-op/gocron: with the
# module paths as they are, gocron's mocks/ cloned the repository and extracted v2.2.10
# again. So both are given each path without its /vN suffix, and on github.com,
# gitlab.com and bitbucket.org also its first three elements, which is the root there
# even for a repository whose modules are all nested. Only a refusal naming one of those
# roots or one of the module paths exactly (`public <path>;`, as Go words it) counts; any
# other is an ordinary failure. The module path too, because for a custom domain Go names
# the path in full: `GOVCS disallows using git for public mvdan.cc/sh/v3`. A module
# refused that way is not re-warmed and is named on stderr; it cannot build offline, but
# `go test ./...` at the root never reaches a nested module, and a faulted package that
# cannot build is refused by gate 9, whose probe compiles every one of them offline. The
# ROOT module refused that way fails the build, because then the task cannot be graded at
# all. Any other failure still means a cold cache. Afterwards nothing but the lock files
# Go takes before a fetch may be there, and those go too, and so does cache/vcs: a clone
# holds the whole upstream history, a refused or half-finished fetch can leave one even
# where a different root let it through, and nothing builds from it offline (the pool
# images have none).
#
# AND NO PROGRAM LINKED FROM THIS REPOSITORY IN THE TREE. termdash ships
# termdashdemo/termdashdemo, an ELF upstream committed and built from the termdash
# packages the faults are in. Every ELF file in the tree, tracked or not, that carries
# one of the tree's module paths followed by a byte that cannot continue the path (gate
# 9's rule, except that a dot always ends it here) is deleted and named on stderr. Only
# paths whose first element has a dot count: a bare nested module name such as `app` is
# in many programs that have nothing to do with this repository. Gate 9 refuses every
# such file anyway; deleting it is what lets the task ship. `cmp` and not a shell `read`,
# because /bin/sh is dash in the Go pool images and dash has no `read -d`.
#
# The first check is that GOCACHE is in the image ENVIRONMENT. This layer runs as root and
# the image ends on USER agent, so if GOCACHE came from HOME instead, root would purge and
# warm /root/.cache/go-build while uid 1000 read /home/agent/.cache/go-build, and the agent
# would start cold with a few hundred megabytes of unreachable cache in the image. All 699
# Go pool Dockerfiles in the store set it and the generator's own test pins it, so this
# cannot fire today; it is here so that a generator that stopped setting it fails the build
# loudly rather than making every Go task quietly slower. `go env` is compared against the
# variable as well, because `go env -w` writes into a config file under HOME, which root
# and the agent do not share either.
#
# The re-warm runs once per module rather than once per tree. `./...` reaches only the
# module it is run in, so a repository with a nested module, or a workspace whose root is
# not itself a module, would have had the purge empty its cache and the re-warm refill
# only part of it, or none of it. Modules are found the way gates.py finds them, by
# walking for go.mod outside vendor and testdata, and read one line at a time rather than
# word split, so a directory with a space in its name is one module and not two. The loop
# body runs in a subshell because of the pipe, which is why a module that will not build
# is carried out on a marker file and not on a variable.
#
# The re-warm is `go install` with GOBIN pointed at a temporary directory, which is the one
# spelling that is safe on every shape a Go module comes in. Measured on go1.23, for a
# module with a main package, a library it imports and a library nothing imports:
#
#   go build ./...            compiles all 391 cache entries, but writes its executable
#                             INTO THE TREE when ./... is a single main package
#   go build -o "$out" ./...  writes nothing to the tree, warms 386, and FAILS OUTRIGHT on
#                             a library-only module with "no main packages to build",
#                             which is most of the pool and would ship a cold cache
#   GOBIN="$out" go install   writes nothing to the tree on any shape, warms 385, and is
#                             happy with a module that has no main package at all
#
# The cache DIRECTORY is put back before the chown, on every path. Without that, a tree
# that does not build in full took the cold branch, the `rm -rf` there left
# /workspace/.cache/go-build absent, and the chown had nothing to chown. The parent is
# root owned in every pool image, because the pool makes /workspace/.cache as root and
# chowns only go-build, so uid 1000 could not recreate it: `go build` as the agent died
# with "failed to initialize build cache at /workspace/.cache/go-build: mkdir ...
# permission denied", and so did the grader's `go test`, which runs as the agent too. A
# cold cache is meant to cost a rollout time, not to make the image unable to compile at
# all. Found on gengo-25e2208e-blind30, where gate 9 refused because its own probe could
# not create the directory either.
#
# A library-only module therefore leaves GOBIN empty, which is the healthy result and not
# a re-warm that did nothing. The 6 entries `go install` leaves for later are link actions
# for main packages, seconds of work against the 100 seconds a cold compile costs every
# rollout and every grading pass, and the grader never asks for them because every `go`
# invocation in it is `go test`. The re-warm writes nothing into the tree.
